---
description: General code style - no classes, named functions, factory pattern, structured errors, semantic and accessible markup
alwaysApply: true
---

- Do not use classes. Use plain functions, closures, and objects instead.
- Prefer named function declarations (`function foo()`) over arrow-assigned variables for top-level definitions.
- For stateful modules, use a factory function that returns an object of methods closing over local variables rather than a `class` with `this`.
- Function components as `export function Name(){}` (no default exports).
- Use SCSS files for styling, organized by feature folder. No TypeScript style objects.
- Use semantic HTML and make every control accessible: labelled form controls, real `button` and `a` elements, table headers, and alternative text (`alt`) for informative images. Use `aria-*` and `role` only where native semantics are not enough.
- Never use abbreviations or acronyms in variable names, function names, or type names. Every name must be fully descriptive so a human can understand its meaning at a glance. Code is read far more than it is written - optimise for cognitive ease, not keystroke economy. `slotWithPosition` not `swp`, `aspectRatio` not `ar`, `connector` not `conn`, `repository` not `repo`, `identifier` not `id`, `webAddress` not `url`.
  - Names fixed by a library or the language (`Promise`, `JSON`, a library's own export) are fine. MongoDB-style `_id` does not apply here.

## Errors

- Throw `Error` objects so stack traces are kept. Never throw strings.
- Classes are banned, so structured errors come from a factory in `shared/`:

```typescript
throw createApplicationError("observationNotFound", "The observation does not exist", { observationIdentifier });
```

- `createApplicationError(code, message, details?)` returns an `Error` carrying a stable `code` string and optional `details`. Callers branch on `code`, never on message text.

## Extracting logic

- When a block of logic has a clear meaning, extract it into a named function. Name the function after what it does, not how it does it.
- If that helper is only used inside one parent function, nest it there as a local function declaration. Nesting makes the scope obvious: the helper exists only for that parent, and is not a reusable module export.

```typescript
// BAD - helper looks like a shared module export, even though only one caller uses it
export function observationHasMeasuredValue(observation: Observation): boolean {
  return Number.isFinite(observation.value);
}

export function claimHasMeasuredValues(observations: Observation[]): boolean {
  return observations.every(observationHasMeasuredValue);
}

// GOOD - helper is nested, so its scope and purpose are obvious
export function claimHasMeasuredValues(observations: Observation[]): boolean {
  return observations.every(observationHasMeasuredValue);

  function observationHasMeasuredValue(observation: Observation): boolean {
    return Number.isFinite(observation.value);
  }
}
```


## Function parameters

Do not define functions with multiple positional parameters like this:

```typescript
log(level: LogLevel, event: string, fields?: Record<string, string | number | boolean | null>): void;
```

At the call site, it is not clear what each value represents:

```typescript
logger.log("info", "backendStopping", { signal });
```

Instead, define the function with one destructured object parameter:

```typescript
type LogParams = {
  level: LogLevel;
  event: string;
  fields?: Record<string, string | number | boolean | null>;
}
export type Logger = {
  log({level, event, fields}: LogParams): void;
}
```

This makes the call site look like this:

```typescript
logger.log({
  level: "info",
  event: "backendStopping",
  fields: { signal },
});
```

This makes it easy to see at a glance that `"info"` is the level and `"backendStopping"` is the event.
